def paragraph(prefix,new,reason):
    found=re.findall(r"(?m)^"+re.escape(prefix)+r".*?(?=\n\n|\n\\end\{abstract\}|\Z)",source,flags=re.S)
    assert len(found)==1,(prefix,len(found))
    old=found[0].strip()
    ops.append(dict(old=old,new=new,reason=reason))
def literal(old,new,reason):
    assert source.count(old)==1,(old,source.count(old))
    ops.append(dict(old=old,new=new,reason=reason))
paragraph("This paper investigates",r"This paper investigates the asset pricing implications of investor-manager interactions on digital platforms. We develop the Effective Sentiment Index (ESI) to combine investor and managerial sentiment within an interactive dialogue. This index extracts a pricing signal by evaluating substantive managerial optimism conditional on the initial investor state and capturing market concerns reflected in question volume and inquiry tone. Using Q\&A dialogues from Chinese interactive platforms, an equally weighted portfolio that buys high ESI stocks and sells low ESI stocks earns an annualized gross return of 10.56\% after replies become public. Its risk adjusted return is positive under Chinese CH4 and FF3 models. ESI predicts subsequent returns with firm controls, but the incremental evidence weakens when competing textual signals enter jointly. Our study sheds light on the pricing effect of the substantive content in interactions between investors and managers.","Approved public availability C2C baseline replaces the old VW 14.5 percent headline.")
paragraph("Using IIP records covering",r"Using IIP records covering 2,489,503 investor questions for 3,600 Chinese A-share firms from 2010 to 2024, our empirical analysis proceeds in two steps. First, we compute continuous sentiment and substantiveness measures from the IIP dialogues. Investor inquiries are more negative and more dispersed than managerial replies. Using 0.5 as a descriptive cutoff, 66.6\% of active firm-days contain substantive replies. Second, we conduct portfolio sorts after managerial replies become public. All three signals use the same candidate stock pool and complete return dates. The equally weighted ESI strategy earns an annualized gross return of 10.56\%, a Sharpe ratio of 1.11, and a CH4 alpha of 4.68 bps. The portfolio weighted by market capitalization earns 7.13\% annually, with a CH4 alpha of 3.78 bps that is not statistically significant. Managerial tone also predicts positive returns, and these comparisons do not establish a consistent advantage for ESI over both tones. Panel OLS and Fama and MacBeth estimates for ESI remain positive and nominally significant at horizons from one through sixty trading days. Its coefficient is not significant in the full text horse race. The earlier question date O2C comparisons are retained in the appendix.","Reconcile headline portfolio, horizon, and incremental results without retuning.")
paragraph("Second, we contribute to asset pricing research",r"Second, we contribute to asset pricing research by evaluating the pricing value of dialogues between investors and managers. Previous research on interactive platforms focuses on how aggregate participation affects the information environment and corporate decisions \citep{lee2022shall,xu2024}. Our study shifts the focus to stock return prediction. We distinguish the date on which an investor asks a question from the date on which its managerial reply becomes public. ESI predicts returns after public reply availability when entered with firm controls, but its coefficient is not significant after the component text measures enter jointly. This comparison clarifies the scope of the pricing information in the matched dialogue.","Contribution is the tested design, not unsupported incremental significance.")
paragraph("Third, we use the interaction context",r"Third, we use the interaction context to refine the interpretation of managerial sentiment. \citet{jiang2019} argue that aggregate sentiment from corporate disclosures reflects managerial overconfidence and extrapolative beliefs. We extend research on managerial sentiment by evaluating managerial tone when listed firms respond to explicit public inquiries. Managerial reply tone predicts subsequent returns after replies become public. The historical conditional sorts describe how this association varies with reply substantiveness and preceding investor sentiment under the question date convention. They motivate the interaction features but do not establish these patterns under every information date rule.","Keep conditional interpretation explicitly tied to its unchanged historical specification.")
paragraph("Replies may be posted",r"Replies may be posted after the original question date. We therefore distinguish the date to which a dialogue belongs from the date on which all its replies are public. The source data retain both posting dates. We first aggregate the textual scores by firm and original question date, keeping investor tone linked to the associated managerial replies. The primary return panel then assigns each completed cohort to its public availability date rather than using replies that are not yet observed on the question date. Historical comparisons retain the original question date aggregation.","Primary timing changed with user approval, existing score matching retained.")
paragraph("We construct a conservative availability test.",r"We use a conservative public availability convention. Because the authoritative textual scores are available for each original firm and question date cohort, we assign each completed cohort to the first exchange trading date strictly after its last recorded reply date, denoted by $a$. Several cohorts available for one firm on that day are aggregated into one observation before calculating ESI. The primary one day outcome is the total excess return from close $a$ to close $a+1$. This rule prevents a managerial reply from entering ESI before it is public and aligns the holding interval with daily risk factors. The resulting availability panel contains 516,522 observations from 2017 through 2024 before the return and control completeness requirements. Among answered interactions, the median reply delay is 2.76 days and the 90th percentile is 17.01 days. Appendix Table \ref{tab:horizon_robustness} preserves the earlier O2C timing comparison.","Do not rename cached O2C outcome as C2C. New estimates were recomputed.")
paragraph("Our primary dependent variable is",r"Our primary dependent variable is the total excess return from close $a$ to close $a+1$. We use CSMAR stock returns with cash dividends reinvested and subtract the risk free return for the same holding day. At longer horizons, we compound stock returns and risk free returns separately from close $a$ to close $a+h$, requiring an observed return on every holding day. CH4 and domestic FF3 portfolio factors cover the corresponding full daily holding intervals. The primary return regressions use excess returns rather than residuals from an ex post factor regression. Historical open to close (O2C) results are supplementary because conventional daily factors also include the overnight interval \citep{aboody2018,lopez2023chatgpt}.","Actual return units and cumulative compounding.")
paragraph("To evaluate the return predictability",r"To evaluate the return predictability of interactive communication, we form daily portfolios sorted on each textual signal after public reply availability. ESI, investor tone, and managerial tone use the same daily candidate stock pool. For directional measures, we require long candidates to exhibit positive tone and short candidates to exhibit negative tone. We restrict the long and short candidate pools to the upper and lower 30 percent of the daily signal distribution. Following the portfolio design in \citet{ke2021predicting}, we select up to 50 firms with the most extreme values in each leg. The fixed number keeps portfolio breadth comparable across days with different levels of platform activity. Returns run from close $a$ to close $a+1$. We report equal weight (EW) and value weight (VW) long minus short returns, annualized performance, and alphas from CH4 and domestic FF3. Value weights use lagged market capitalization. The common comparison uses 1,786 complete return dates from 2017 through 2024. The short leg is theoretical, and all returns are gross of trading and borrowing costs. Section \ref{sec:robustness_checks} reports execution and cost sensitivity. \footnote{The primary risk benchmark is the Liu-Stambaugh-Yuan four-factor model (CH4) \citep{liu2019}. Domestic FF3 uses CSMAR THRFACDAY market P9714 and its circulating market capitalization series RiskPremium1, SMB1, and HML1 \citep{fama1993common}. The original factor file records decimal daily returns, which we do not divide by 100. Historical q5 comparisons use the United States Global-q database, not a Chinese q5 model.}","Joint pool and exact holding interval replace unequal historical ranking samples.")
paragraph("Table \\ref{tab:horse_race} reports",r"Table \ref{tab:horse_race} reports the performance of portfolios sorted on ESI and the alternative textual signals. In Panel A, under equal weighting, ESI earns 4.15 bps per day, an annualized return of 10.56\%, and a Sharpe ratio of 1.11. Its CH4 alpha is 4.68 bps ($t=3.29$), and its domestic FF3 alpha is 4.33 bps ($t=3.09$). In Panel B, under value weighting, ESI earns 2.85 bps per day, or 7.13\% annually. Its CH4 alpha is 3.78 bps ($t=1.54$), and its FF3 alpha is 3.09 bps ($t=1.27$). Neither value weight alpha is statistically significant. Managerial tone earns CH4 alphas of 4.75 and 6.85 bps under the two weighting methods. Thus, ESI has a positive gross return association, but these common pool comparisons do not establish superiority over managerial tone. Appendix Table \ref{tab:common_pool_spanning} reports direct comparisons of the strategy alphas.","New verified CH4/FF3 common date outputs.")
