paragraph("\\noindent where $R_{i,t+1",r"\noindent where day $t$ denotes the public availability date $a$ in the primary panel. $R_{i,t+1 \to t+h}$ is the cumulative total stock return minus the cumulative risk free return from close $a$ to close $a+h$, with $h \in \{1,5,10,20,40,60\}$. The controls $\mathbf{X}_{i,t}$ include firm size, the book to market ratio, return on assets, leverage, Amihud illiquidity, turnover, the preceding stock return, return volatility, prior cumulative return, historical media tone, and a news coverage indicator. The coefficient vector on these controls is $\boldsymbol{\delta}$. Firm and date fixed effects are denoted by $\mu_i$ and $\tau_t$. ESI and the two tone measures use their historical calibration scales. Other predictors are centered and scaled within the availability panel without using returns.","No ex post q5 residual outcome in primary regression.")
paragraph("Panel A of Table \\ref{tab:panel_horse_race}",r"Panel A of Table \ref{tab:panel_horse_race} reports the panel OLS horse race for the next day return on a common sample of 377,721 observations from 2017 through 2024. ESI predicts 1.48 bps when entered with the baseline controls ($t=4.36$). The full specification adds investor tone, managerial tone, question volume, reply substantiveness, question length, reply length, and Q\&A similarity. Its ESI coefficient is 0.58 bps ($t=0.75$), and adjusted $R^2$ does not increase. The public availability horse race therefore does not establish incremental predictability beyond the complete text benchmark.","Actual full horse race, no feature selection.")
paragraph("Panel B reports the panel OLS estimates",r"Panel B reports the panel OLS estimates across return horizons. A one historical standard deviation increase in ESI predicts 1.48 bps over one day ($t=4.36$), 6.63 bps over ten days ($t=5.57$), and 10.29 bps over sixty days ($t=2.94$). The separate ESI estimates are positive and nominally significant at all six horizons. Panel C reports Fama and MacBeth regressions \citep{fama1973risk}. We estimate a cross-sectional regression on each eligible trading day and average the resulting coefficients. The ESI coefficient is 1.00 bps over one day ($t=2.86$) and 18.90 bps over sixty days ($t=4.07$). HAC standard errors use at least five exchange trading day lags and increase with the return horizon. These correlated horizon estimates do not represent six independent discoveries.","Recomputed compounded C2C horizon estimates.")
paragraph("Taken together, the panel OLS",r"Taken together, the panel OLS and Fama and MacBeth estimates show a positive association between ESI and subsequent excess returns after replies become public. The full horse race does not establish an incremental coefficient beyond the competing textual signals. These estimates show a predictive association, not its economic mechanism. Section \ref{sec:fundamental_channel} examines subsequent fundamental news, while Section \ref{sec:microstructure_oib} reports the supplementary trading diagnostic.","Limits agree with new primary results.")
paragraph("Appendix Table \\ref{tab:grid_search_summary} summarizes",r"Appendix Table \ref{tab:grid_search_summary} summarizes the distribution rules used to calibrate ESI. The four parameters are functions of the 2010 to 2016 text distribution and do not use return outcomes. Appendix Tables \ref{tab:horse_race_legacy} and \ref{tab:panel_horse_race_legacy} preserve the original question date O2C portfolio and regression comparisons. Appendix Table \ref{tab:horizon_robustness} preserves the O2C information timing check. Incremental ESI significance in the question date panel is not reproduced in the current public availability C2C horse race. The same-day and single-reply-date restrictions reduce the sample and do not produce significant incremental coefficients in those archived O2C tests.","Explicit historical supplement is not a primary robustness certification.")
paragraph("Our empirical analysis documents the return predictability",r"Our empirical analysis documents a positive return association for ESI after replies become public. The equally weighted portfolio earns 4.15 basis points per day and a CH4 alpha of 4.68 basis points. The value weight alpha is positive but not statistically significant. Separate panel and Fama and MacBeth estimates remain positive and nominally significant from one through sixty trading days. ESI is not significant after the component text measures enter jointly. The evidence therefore supports an association with returns, but not a consistent incremental signal or an advantage over both standalone tone strategies.","Conclusion reconciles new primary estimates.")
